"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,928\nThe Learning Brain\n\n2\n00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:07,473\nThe parietal lobe: space, attention and number\n\n3\n00:00:10,473 --> 00:00:17,566\nIn 1978, two neurologists in Milan asked two patients to picture the city's cathedral square.\n\n4\n00:00:18,102 --> 00:00:20,216\nImagining they faced the cathedral,\n\n5\n00:00:20,474 --> 00:00:23,207\nboth described only the buildings on their right.\n\n6\n00:00:23,783 --> 00:00:26,717\nThen they imagined standing on the cathedral steps,\n\n7\n00:00:26,813 --> 00:00:27,496\nlooking back.\n\n8\n00:00:28,056 --> 00:00:30,549\nNow they named the buildings they had left out,\n\n9\n00:00:30,756 --> 00:00:33,031\nand forgot the ones they had just described.\n\n10\n00:00:35,201 --> 00:00:38,336\nBoth patients had damage to the right side of the brain.\n\n11\n00:00:38,892 --> 00:00:43,535\nThis kind of neglect most often follows damage to the right parietal lobe,\n\n12\n00:00:43,820 --> 00:00:45,676\nat the top and back of the brain.\n\n13\n00:00:46,262 --> 00:00:48,562\nPeople ignore the left side of space,\n\n14\n00:00:48,780 --> 00:00:50,704\neven in a scene they remember.\n\n15\n00:00:53,874 --> 00:00:56,452\nThe parietal lobe helps steer attention.\n\n16\n00:00:56,924 --> 00:00:58,657\nA groove along its side,\n\n17\n00:00:58,739 --> 00:01:04,462\nthe intraparietal sulcus, works with the frontal lobe to point your attention where you choose.\n\n18\n00:01:04,835 --> 00:01:06,651\nMagicians exploit this.\n\n19\n00:01:07,008 --> 00:01:12,873\nIn one study, only 2 of 20 viewers noticed a magician drop a cigarette in full view.\n\n20\n00:01:15,043 --> 00:01:17,191\nThe same groove helps you sense number.\n\n21\n00:01:17,538 --> 00:01:18,727\nGlance at three dots,\n\n22\n00:01:18,747 --> 00:01:19,948\nand you just know.\n\n23\n00:01:20,374 --> 00:01:21,862\nThis is called subitizing,\n\n24\n00:01:21,882 --> 00:01:23,837\nand it works up to about four.\n\n25\n00:01:24,260 --> 00:01:26,885\nBeyond that, you have to count or estimate.\n\n26\n00:01:27,340 --> 00:01:32,961\nIn brain scans, the intraparietal sulcus responds when the number of dots changes.\n\n27\n00:01:35,631 --> 00:01:38,178\nChildren also lay numbers out in space,\n\n28\n00:01:38,389 --> 00:01:40,348\nbut at first the map is squashed.\n\n29\n00:01:40,819 --> 00:01:43,229\nOn a line from 0 to 1000,\n\n30\n00:01:43,375 --> 00:01:47,857\nmost second graders spread small numbers out and crowd large ones together.\n\n31\n00:01:48,290 --> 00:01:52,458\nSome even put 150 closer to 1000 than to 0.\n\n32\n00:01:52,954 --> 00:01:56,653\nBy sixth grade, most children space numbers evenly.\n\n33\n00:01:58,323 --> 00:01:59,952\nPractice straightens the line.\n\n34\n00:02:00,475 --> 00:02:05,772\nIn one study, preschoolers from low-income families played a board game numbered 1 to 10,\n\n35\n00:02:06,044 --> 00:02:08,273\nfor four 15-minute sessions.\n\n36\n00:02:08,704 --> 00:02:10,757\nTheir counting, number naming,\n\n37\n00:02:10,841 --> 00:02:14,225\ncomparing and number-line estimates all improved.\n\n38\n00:02:14,646 --> 00:02:19,285\nChildren who played the same game with colors instead of numbers did not improve.\n\n39\n00:02:20,955 --> 00:02:22,756\nWhy does this matter for learning?\n\n40\n00:02:23,126 --> 00:02:25,107\nNumber sense is partly spatial.\n\n41\n00:02:25,512 --> 00:02:31,185\nNumber lines, board games and rulers give children an even map of numbers to build on.\n\n42\n00:02:31,455 --> 00:02:33,343\nAnd because attention is limited,\n\n43\n00:02:33,576 --> 00:02:35,990\nlearners can miss what is in plain view.\n\n44\n00:02:36,427 --> 00:02:37,864\nPoint to what matters.\n\n45\n00:02:40,034 --> 00:02:42,291\nYour parietal lobe maps space,\n\n46\n00:02:42,483 --> 00:02:45,670\nsteers attention, and lays numbers out on a line.\n\n"
